Git & GitHub Lab Programs — Step-by-Step Guide

Prerequisites

Install:

Git

A GitHub account


Verify Git installation:

git --version

Configure Git (first time only):

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"


---

1. Initialize a new Git repository in a folder named practice-repo and make your first commit

Step 1: Create folder

mkdir practice-repo
cd practice-repo

Step 2: Initialize Git repository

git init

Explanation:

Creates a hidden .git folder

Git now starts tracking this project



---

Step 3: Create a sample file

echo "Hello Git" > sample.txt


---

Step 4: Check status

git status

You will see:

sample.txt

as an untracked file.


---

Step 5: Add file to staging area

git add sample.txt


---

Step 6: Commit the file

git commit -m "First commit"

Explanation:

-m means commit message



---

Step 7: Verify commit

git log


---

2. Create a local project, connect it to a newly created GitHub repository, and push the project files

Step 1: Create project folder

mkdir myproject
cd myproject
git init


---

Step 2: Create files

echo "My Project" > README.md


---

Step 3: Add and commit

git add .
git commit -m "Initial project commit"


---

Step 4: Create repository on GitHub

Go to:

GitHub New Repository Page

Create repository:

Repository Name: myproject

Keep it Public or Private


Do NOT initialize with README.


---

Step 5: Connect local repo to GitHub

Copy repository URL and run:

git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/myproject.git

Check remote:

git remote -v


---

Step 6: Push files

git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

Explanation:

-u sets upstream branch



---

3. Make a change to a file in GitHub repository and pull updated version locally

Step 1: Edit file on GitHub

Open repository

Click README.md

Edit and save changes



---

Step 2: Pull changes locally

Inside local repository:

git pull origin main

Explanation:

Downloads latest changes

Updates local files



---

Step 3: Verify

cat README.md


---

4. Clone a GitHub repository and verify project files

Step 1: Copy repository URL

Example:

https://github.com/USERNAME/myproject.git


---

Step 2: Clone repository

git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/myproject.git


---

Step 3: Move into folder

cd myproject


---

Step 4: Verify files

ls

or on Windows:

dir


---

5. Clone repository, create new file, and commit locally

Step 1: Clone

git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/myproject.git
cd myproject


---

Step 2: Create new file

echo "New File" > notes.txt


---

Step 3: Add file

git add notes.txt


---

Step 4: Commit

git commit -m "Added notes file"


---

Step 5: Verify commit

git log


---

6. Create a new branch, make changes, and commit

Step 1: Create branch

git branch feature1


---

Step 2: Switch branch

git checkout feature1

Shortcut:

git checkout -b feature1


---

Step 3: Edit file

echo "Feature work" >> README.md


---

Step 4: Add and commit

git add README.md
git commit -m "Updated README in feature1 branch"


---

Step 5: Verify current branch

git branch

Current branch will have *


---

7. Create two branches from main and merge both

Step 1: Switch to main

git checkout main


---

Step 2: Create branch1

git checkout -b branch1

Make change:

echo "Branch1 work" >> file.txt

Commit:

git add .
git commit -m "Changes from branch1"


---

Step 3: Create branch2 from main

git checkout main
git checkout -b branch2

Make changes:

echo "Branch2 work" >> file2.txt

Commit:

git add .
git commit -m "Changes from branch2"


---

Step 4: Merge branch1

git checkout main
git merge branch1


---

Step 5: Merge branch2

git merge branch2


---

Step 6: Verify history

git log --oneline --graph


---

8. Create feature branch, update file, commit, and merge

Step 1: Create feature branch

git checkout -b feature-login


---

Step 2: Update file

echo "Login feature added" >> app.txt


---

Step 3: Commit

git add .
git commit -m "Added login feature"


---

Step 4: Merge into main

git checkout main
git merge feature-login


---

Step 5: Push changes

git push origin main


---

9. Fork a public repository, clone fork locally, and check remotes

Step 1: Fork repository

Open any public repository and click Fork.

Example public repository: First Contributions Repository


---

Step 2: Clone your fork

git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/first-contributions.git


---

Step 3: Enter repository

cd first-contributions


---

Step 4: Check remotes

git remote -v

Output:

origin  https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/first-contributions.git


---

10. Fork repository, make local change, commit, and push

Step 1: Edit file

echo "My contribution" >> contribution.txt


---

Step 2: Add file

git add contribution.txt


---

Step 3: Commit

git commit -m "Added contribution"


---

Step 4: Push to fork

git push origin main


---

11. Add upstream repository and pull latest changes

Step 1: Add upstream remote

git remote add upstream https://github.com/ORIGINAL-OWNER/first-contributions.git


---

Step 2: Verify remotes

git remote -v

You will see:

origin
upstream


---

Step 3: Fetch latest changes

git fetch upstream


---

Step 4: Pull updates

git checkout main
git pull upstream main


---

12. Create branch in cloned repository, make changes, merge, and push

Step 1: Create branch

git checkout -b update-docs


---

Step 2: Make changes

echo "Documentation updated" >> README.md


---

Step 3: Commit changes

git add README.md
git commit -m "Updated documentation"


---

Step 4: Merge branch

git checkout main
git merge update-docs


---

Step 5: Push final version

git push origin main


---

Important Git Commands Summary

Command	Purpose

git init	Initialize repository
git clone	Copy repository
git status	Show current status
git add	Add files to staging
git commit	Save changes
git push	Upload changes
git pull	Download latest changes
git branch	List branches
git checkout	Switch branch
git merge	Merge branches
git remote -v	Show remotes



---

Basic Git Workflow

Working Directory
       ↓
git add
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
git commit
       ↓
Local Repository
       ↓
git push
       ↓
GitHub Repository


---

Common Errors and Fixes

1. Permission Denied

Use GitHub authentication:

Personal Access Token

GitHub CLI

SSH keys


GitHub Docs: GitHub Authentication Docs


---

2. Merge Conflict

Git will show conflict markers:

<<<<<<< HEAD
Your code
=======
Other code
>>>>>>> branch

Fix manually, then:

git add .
git commit


---

3. Remote Already Exists

git remote remove origin
git remote add origin URL
